  "id": "Compound::DB00420 - Gene::1131",
  "verbalization": "Promazine is likely to interact with Chrm3 through a mechanism involving the G-protein coupled receptor pathway. The evidence suggests that Promazine binds to Chrm4, which is a component of the G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptor activity or neurotransmitter receptor activity. These pathways are involved in the regulation of muscarinic and acetylcholine receptor functions, which are known to be implicated in various physiological processes. The presence of Chrm3 as a participant in these receptor activities implies that Promazine may influence the function of Chrm3 through its interaction with Chrm4. This relationship is supported by the consistent evidence across multiple paths, highlighting a potential role for Promazine in modulating Chrm3 activity via the G-protein coupled receptor system.",
